# env, never committed, in any environment.
#
# `.env` used to be tracked with local-development defaults, which was fine
# until the production deploy told you to write your real secrets into that
# same path: `git pull` would then refuse to run, or worse, somebody would
# commit a database password to a public repository. One filename cannot be
# both "safe defaults everyone shares" and "the secrets on this machine".
#
# Local development: cp .env.example .env
# Production:       cp .env.production.example .env
.env
.env.local
.env*.local

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem
.vercel
*.tsbuildinfo

# logs
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*
screenshots/
